  date = {2026},
  abstract = {Across even Latin America's most stable liberal democracies, rising insecurity and persistent economic hardship have coincided with greater public openness to leaders who promise rapid "results" while sidestepping institutional checks and civil liberties. An examination of these preferences can offer valuable insights into when democratic publics may tolerate an anti-democratic executive in exchange for effective governance, thus jeopardizing democratic health and longevity. We study how citizens evaluate trade-offs between government effectiveness and respect for democratic institutions. We implement a conjoint experiment in which respondents compare pairs of hypothetical governments and choose which they would prefer to govern their country. Each government profile varies along three dimensions: (i) the policy domain prioritized (crime, poverty, or public services), (ii) the level of effectiveness in addressing that domain, and (iii) the institutional strategy used to govern. The central objective is to estimate whether citizens reward effective governments, whether they penalize democratic violations, and whether tolerance for such violations depends on the policy domain at stake. The experiment is embedded in a cross-national survey of approximately 13,200 respondents across six countries (Brazil, Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica, Mexico, and Peru), fielded by IPSOS in March 2026.},
