  date = {2026},
  abstract = {How do citizens engage with a predatory arm of the state? We answer this by elaborating a theory of channel reconfiguration. We argue that predation pushes citizens away from direct interactions with the state and towards intermediated ones, largely for reasons related to safety and trust. This paper demonstrates how channel reconfiguration works by showing that police violence (1) discourages reporting to the police, while (2) shifting reporting towards intermediate channels. We focus on the reporting of gender-based violence (GBV), which is responsive to citizen perceptions of the police. To estimate the relationship between police violence and GBV reporting in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, we build a database containing millions of police records and call logs from an intermediary. Our analyses demonstrate that exposure to police violence is associated with an absolute and relative decline in reporting to the police visa -vis the hotline. We then estimate the causal effect of an exogenous decrease in access to the anonymous hotline. GBV reports to the hotline fell by 45\%, but fewer than half of these estimated callers filed a police report instead. We substantiate both quantitative exercises with interview and cross-national survey evidence to argue that low trust in a predatory police force is driving this reporting behavior. Together, these findings demonstrate that police violence incentivizes citizens to report GBV to alternative channels or, in their absence, to stay silent. * We are grateful to},
  file = {/Users/tarahein/Zotero/storage/QLGLC5NJ/under-the-radar_povgov.pdf}
}

